{#- The one template behind the four industry pages.

    Each page under src/pages/ is a few lines of front matter, a `set` that
    points `sector` at its own data file, and an include of this. Nunjucks
    includes see the caller's variables, so `sector` resolves here; index.njk
    and pages/home.njk work the same way. Four copies of this markup would
    have drifted the first time one was edited, which is the reason the
    How It Works partial exists, and it applies here just as well.

    The sections, top to bottom, are the shapes the technology pages already
    use, so nothing here needed new CSS beyond the selector additions noted
    in styles.css:

      1. Hero: the NaturalDetect hero, eyebrow / name / one-liner / intro /
         two buttons, photograph in the 4:3 panel with the same crop controls.
      2. Two cards: the customer's problem first, then how Natural Trace
         helps. The Copy Guide's brief for the Industries pages is "lead with
         customer problems before technology", so the challenge card is on
         the left.
      3. In practice: the use cases from usecases.yml whose sector the data
         file names. Rendered from the one source rather than copied, and
         omitted entirely when nothing matches, which is Functional Foods
         today. An empty band with a heading and no cards would read as a
         mistake.
      4. Validated product formats, in the dark band, the per-sector subset
         of the NaturalTag matrices list.
      5. The closing call to action.

    How It Works is deliberately not included. It would sit directly against
    the dark formats band, and the assessment call to action is already in
    the hero and at the foot of the page, which is where Alex Wild asked for
    it. -#}
<section class="challenge hero-industry" style="padding-top:9rem">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="challenge-grid">
      <div class="challenge-text">
        <span class="section-label">{{ sector.hero.label }}</span>
        <h2 class="section-title">{{ sector.hero.title }}</h2>
        <p class="section-subtitle">{{ sector.hero.tagline }}</p>
        <p class="challenge-lead" style="margin-top:1.5rem">{{ sector.hero.intro }}</p>
        <div style="display:flex;gap:1rem;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-top:2rem">
          <a href="{{ sector.hero.primary_url }}" class="btn btn-primary" style="cursor:pointer">{{ sector.hero.primary_text }}</a>
          <a href="{{ sector.hero.secondary_url }}" class="btn btn-line" style="cursor:pointer">{{ sector.hero.secondary_text }}</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      {# The same crop controls as the technology pages, so an editor can move
         or tighten the picture from the panel without a new file. #}
      <div class="solution-photo">
        <img src="{{ sector.hero.image }}" alt="{{ sector.hero.image_alt }}" loading="lazy"
          style="{% if sector.hero.image_position %}object-position:{{ sector.hero.image_position }};{% endif %}{% if sector.hero.image_zoom %}--photo-zoom:{{ sector.hero.image_zoom }};--photo-zoom-origin:{{ sector.hero.image_position or "center" }}{% endif %}">
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="valueprop">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="vp-grid about-grid-2">
      <article class="vp-card">
        <h3>{{ sector.challenge.title }}</h3>
        <ul class="sol-list" style="grid-template-columns:1fr;margin-top:0">
          {% for item in sector.challenge.items %}<li>{{ item }}</li>{% endfor %}
        </ul>
      </article>
      <article class="vp-card">
        <h3>{{ sector.value.title }}</h3>
        <ul class="sol-list" style="grid-template-columns:1fr;margin-top:0">
          {% for item in sector.value.items %}<li>{{ item }}</li>{% endfor %}
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

{#- Collect the matching cases first so the band can be skipped when there are
    none. `set` inside a for loop does not reach the outer scope in Nunjucks,
    but pushing onto an array declared outside it does, which is what the
    odd-looking assignment is for. -#}
{%- set matched = [] -%}
{%- for case in usecases.cases.items -%}
  {%- if sector.use_cases.sectors and (case.sector in sector.use_cases.sectors) -%}
    {%- set matched = (matched.push(case), matched) -%}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endfor -%}
{% if matched.length %}
<section class="usecases">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="vp-head">
      <span class="section-label">{{ sector.use_cases.label }}</span>
      <h2 class="section-title">{{ sector.use_cases.title }}</h2>
    </div>
    {% for case in matched %}
    {% include "partials/usecase.njk" %}
    {% endfor %}
    <p class="section-more"><a href="{{ sector.use_cases.all_url }}">{{ sector.use_cases.all_text }}</a></p>
  </div>
</section>
{% endif %}

<section class="howitworks">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="how-head">
      <span class="section-label">{{ sector.formats.label }}</span>
      <h2 class="section-title">{{ sector.formats.title }}</h2>
      <p style="color:rgba(255,255,255,.65);font-size:.98rem;line-height:1.8">{{ sector.formats.description }}</p>
    </div>
    {# Column count follows the number of groups: three groups fill the
       three-column grid, two would leave a hole in it. #}
    <div class="how-grid {% if sector.formats.items.length == 2 %}how-grid-2{% else %}how-grid-3{% endif %}">
      {% for m in sector.formats.items %}
      <div class="how-step">
        <h3>{{ m.group }}</h3>
        <p>{{ m.items }}</p>
      </div>
      {% endfor %}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="finalcta">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="finalcta-inner">
      <h2>{{ sector.final_cta.title }}</h2>
      <p>{{ sector.final_cta.description }}</p>
      <div class="finalcta-actions">
        <a href="{{ sector.final_cta.primary_url }}" class="btn btn-white" style="cursor:pointer">{{ sector.final_cta.primary_text }}</a>
        <a href="{{ sector.final_cta.secondary_url }}" class="btn btn-ghost" style="cursor:pointer">{{ sector.final_cta.secondary_text }}</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
